# A call on a dynamically-typed receiver must resolve against the class the
# object ACTUALLY is, not against whichever class happened to be registered when
# the call was lowered.
#
# THE AXIS IS IMPORT ORDER, so both orders belong in the fixture. `traffic` is
# imported FIRST, so when Craft.update is lowered the only registered carrier of
# the name `wind` is Craft -- where it is a data field. Environment, which
# declares it as a METHOD, is not in the class table yet: the class table is
# global and cumulative while the registration pre-pass's window is ONE module's
# token span, so a call resolves against the classes compiled SO FAR. The call
# used to be lowered as a hard cast to Craft plus a field read, with no runtime
# class test, so an Environment receiver read Craft's slot and called it:
#   TypeError: object is not callable - the name is None
# Swapping the two import lines below -- changing no code at all -- made the
# identical program correct, and that asymmetry is what identified the mechanism.
# bug-n-a-variant-field-is-claimed-as-the-callee-of-an-open-world-call-with-no-runtime-class-test
#
# A FIXTURE WRITTEN IN THE PASSING ORDER CERTIFIES THE DEFECT, so the failing
# order is the one used here.
from nilpy_callorder import traffic
from nilpy_callorder.environment import Environment

c = traffic.Craft()
print(c.update(Environment()))

# The same call with the result going to a LOCAL instead of to the shadowing
# field. This row WARNED and was correct even before the fix.
print(c.peek(Environment()))

# A name NO class declares either way: the scan finds nothing, warns, and defers
# to run time. That arm was always correct, and it is here because the warning it
# emits was mistaken for this defect's signature -- the failing row above emits
# ZERO warnings while this passing row emits one.
print(c.gusty(Environment()))

# THE MUST-NOT-BREAK ROW. A genuine dispatch table: `cb` is None at birth, armed
# with a module-level def, and the receiver really IS the candidate class, so the
# `is` test matches and the direct arm runs. A fix that sent every call to the
# dynamic dispatcher would pass every row above and lose this one.
print(traffic.through_table())

# The field is still a field: reading it back proves the store landed in Craft's
# own slot and not somewhere the dispatch invented.
print(c.wind)
